1
00:00:01,836 --> 00:00:03,629
Hey.

2
00:00:03,671 --> 00:00:04,546
Hi.

3
00:00:04,588 --> 00:00:06,214
What are you doing?

4
00:00:06,256 --> 00:00:08,425
Well, you know, I was thinking
of moving the couch over here.

5
00:00:08,467 --> 00:00:10,010
Why would you want
to do that?

6
00:00:10,051 --> 00:00:12,638
So that there would be
a decent place for me to sit.

7
00:00:13,722 --> 00:00:14,890
Rach...

8
00:00:14,932 --> 00:00:16,350
There is a decent place...

9
00:00:16,391 --> 00:00:18,226
And your lap does not count.

10
00:00:18,268 --> 00:00:20,980
Okay, come on,
help me move this.

11
00:00:21,062 --> 00:00:22,857
No, no, no.

12
00:00:22,898 --> 00:00:24,316
No?

13
00:00:24,358 --> 00:00:26,234
No. Rosita does not move.

14
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:29,864
I'm sorry.

15
00:00:29,905 --> 00:00:30,865
Rosita?

16
00:00:30,906 --> 00:00:31,866
As in...?

17
00:00:31,907 --> 00:00:33,367
As in Rosita does not move.

18
00:00:33,408 --> 00:00:34,785
Joey, it's
just a chair.

19
00:00:34,827 --> 00:00:36,620
What's the big deal?

20
00:00:36,662 --> 00:00:38,956
The big deal is that
it is the exact equal distance

21
00:00:38,998 --> 00:00:41,834
from the bathroom to the kitchen
and it's at the perfect angle

22
00:00:41,876 --> 00:00:44,085
so you don't get any glare
coming off of Stevie.

23
00:00:45,671 --> 00:00:48,089
Stevie the TV?

24
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:51,176
Is there a problem?

25
00:00:51,218 --> 00:00:53,261
No.

26
00:00:55,389 --> 00:00:56,974
Oh, what does he know?

27
00:00:57,016 --> 00:00:58,350
Come on, Rosita.

28
00:00:58,392 --> 00:01:00,394
Us chicas got
to stick together.

29
00:01:02,396 --> 00:01:04,189
( grunting )

30
00:01:04,231 --> 00:01:05,440
Oh!

31
00:01:05,482 --> 00:01:06,608
( gasps )

32
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:10,696
You bitch.

33
00:01:13,281 --> 00:01:18,328
[Captioning sponsored by
WARNER BROS. TELEVISION]

34
00:01:18,370 --> 00:01:23,458
♪ So no one told you
life was gonna be this way ♪

35
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:25,878
♪ Your job's a joke,
you're broke ♪

36
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:28,255
♪ Your love life's D.O.A. ♪

37
00:01:28,296 --> 00:01:32,927
♪ It's like you're always
stuck in second gear ♪

38
00:01:32,968 --> 00:01:35,220
♪ When it hasn't been
your day, your week ♪

39
00:01:35,262 --> 00:01:38,515
♪ Your month,
or even your year ♪

40
00:01:38,557 --> 00:01:41,810
♪ But I'll be there for you ♪

41
00:01:41,852 --> 00:01:45,272
♪ 'Cause you're there
for me, too. ♪

42
00:01:51,110 --> 00:01:52,863
Hey, Ross, so, I was
checking out

43
00:01:52,905 --> 00:01:54,322
the real estate
section...

44
00:01:54,364 --> 00:01:55,323
Yeah?

45
00:01:55,365 --> 00:01:56,658
Look at this.

46
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:59,202
It looks like
Mom and Dad's house.

47
00:01:59,244 --> 00:02:00,579
It even has a tree

48
00:02:00,621 --> 00:02:02,748
with a broken
limb out front.

49
00:02:02,790 --> 00:02:03,791
And the, uh...

50
00:02:03,832 --> 00:02:05,751
the window
in the attic...

51
00:02:05,793 --> 00:02:07,628
Oh, my God!

52
00:02:07,669 --> 00:02:09,588
What? What happened

53
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:11,256
to the window
in the attic?

54
00:02:12,299 --> 00:02:13,759
I can't believe Mom and Dad

55
00:02:13,801 --> 00:02:15,135
are selling the house.

56
00:02:15,176 --> 00:02:16,929
I can't believe
they didn't even tell us.

57
00:02:16,971 --> 00:02:18,138
I can't believe I still
don't know what happened

58
00:02:18,179 --> 00:02:19,598
to the window
in the attic.

59
00:02:22,142 --> 00:02:23,143
Okay, come on, Joey

60
00:02:23,184 --> 00:02:24,394
I'll buy you a new one, all
right?

61
00:02:24,436 --> 00:02:25,604
We'll go down
to the store
right now

62
00:02:25,646 --> 00:02:27,439
and we'll get you
a new chair.

63
00:02:30,442 --> 00:02:32,402
She's not even cold yet.

64
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:38,408
But don't you think Rosita would
have wanted you to move on?

65
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:42,830
I mean, she did always put
your comfort first.

66
00:02:44,456 --> 00:02:46,458
Well, that's true.

67
00:02:50,004 --> 00:02:52,464
I can't believe we have
to say good-bye

68
00:02:52,506 --> 00:02:54,591
to the house
we grew up in.

69
00:02:54,633 --> 00:02:57,678
Man, some stranger's going
to be living in my room.

70
00:02:57,719 --> 00:03:01,098
Well, after 15 years
of Mom and Dad keeping
it as a shrine to you

71
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:03,182
it's time the
velvet ropes came down.

72
00:03:04,518 --> 00:03:06,185
They kept your room for awhile.

73
00:03:06,227 --> 00:03:07,646
Oh, please.

74
00:03:07,688 --> 00:03:09,356
Dad turned my room
into a gym

75
00:03:09,397 --> 00:03:11,066
20 minutes after
I moved out.

76
00:03:11,108 --> 00:03:12,693
I got to say,
a tanning bed

77
00:03:12,734 --> 00:03:15,362
and a stack of
Victoria's Secret catalogs--

78
00:03:15,403 --> 00:03:16,905
not a gym.

79
00:03:16,947 --> 00:03:18,406
Come on.

80
00:03:18,448 --> 00:03:19,700
You know
they love you.

81
00:03:19,741 --> 00:03:21,200
As much as
they love you?

82
00:03:21,242 --> 00:03:22,577
I was their firstborn.

83
00:03:22,619 --> 00:03:24,329
They thought she was barren.

84
00:03:24,371 --> 00:03:25,539
It's not my fault.

85
00:03:28,291 --> 00:03:29,793
I hate this year.

86
00:03:29,835 --> 00:03:31,712
What's wrong
with this year?

87
00:03:31,753 --> 00:03:33,588
Well, okay, it's
already February

88
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:35,382
and I've only given
two massages

89
00:03:35,423 --> 00:03:38,510
and they were both
the worst tippers in the world.

90
00:03:38,552 --> 00:03:40,095
That was me and Ross.

91
00:03:41,930 --> 00:03:44,808
Oh, that's right.

92
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:47,186
Hey, you know, if you want
to pick up some extra cash

93
00:03:47,227 --> 00:03:49,270
some friends of mine
made good money in college

94
00:03:49,312 --> 00:03:50,564
doing telemarketing.

95
00:03:50,605 --> 00:03:52,440
Yeah, and it'll probably
be better

96
00:03:52,482 --> 00:03:54,275
than the last
telephone job I had.

97
00:03:54,317 --> 00:03:56,862
I probably won't have
to say "spank" as much.

98
00:04:00,281 --> 00:04:02,201
What?

99
00:04:02,242 --> 00:04:05,495
Oh, yeah, like you never called.

100
00:04:12,002 --> 00:04:13,670
So, basically
this is very easy.

101
00:04:13,712 --> 00:04:14,963
You read from
the script

102
00:04:15,005 --> 00:04:16,798
and you try to sell
as much toner

103
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:17,549
as you possibly can.

104
00:04:17,591 --> 00:04:19,218
Okay, I can do that.

105
00:04:19,259 --> 00:04:22,303
Oh, by the way,
I love my office.

106
00:04:22,345 --> 00:04:24,848
Why don't we do
a trial run.

107
00:04:24,890 --> 00:04:26,265
Oh, okay.

108
00:04:26,307 --> 00:04:29,269
Um... all right.

109
00:04:29,310 --> 00:04:33,023
Hi, this is Phoebe
from Empire Office Supplies.

110
00:04:33,065 --> 00:04:35,316
Can I speak to your
supply manager, please?

111
00:04:35,358 --> 00:04:37,027
I'm the supply manager.

112
00:04:37,069 --> 00:04:39,404
Okay, I'd like to talk to you
about your toner needs.

113
00:04:39,446 --> 00:04:40,948
We don't need any toner.

114
00:04:40,989 --> 00:04:42,866
Oh, okay, well, I'm sorry
to bother you. Bye-bye.

115
00:04:42,908 --> 00:04:45,535
Yeah, you're right,
this is easy.

116
00:04:45,577 --> 00:04:48,538
Okay, what was wrong
with that call?

117
00:04:48,580 --> 00:04:50,540
Oh, well, all right.

118
00:04:50,582 --> 00:04:54,128
Um, no offense,
but you were kind of rude.

119
00:04:54,169 --> 00:04:57,131
They're always going to tell you
they don't need toner

120
00:04:57,172 --> 00:04:58,882
but that's okay.

121
00:04:58,924 --> 00:05:01,051
Because whatever they say,
you can find the answer to it

122
00:05:01,093 --> 00:05:02,719
here in
the script.

123
00:05:02,761 --> 00:05:03,929
Oh.

124
00:05:03,971 --> 00:05:05,639
So I think you're
ready to sell toner.

125
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:06,765
Any last questions?

126
00:05:06,807 --> 00:05:07,808
No.

127
00:05:07,849 --> 00:05:09,350
Oh, wait, yes, I do.

128
00:05:09,392 --> 00:05:11,103
I do have one question.

129
00:05:11,145 --> 00:05:12,271
What is toner?

130
00:05:15,149 --> 00:05:16,483
Joey.

131
00:05:18,610 --> 00:05:20,445
Joe?

132
00:05:24,616 --> 00:05:27,494
Full bag?

133
00:05:27,535 --> 00:05:29,537
Beer's still cold.

134
00:05:31,748 --> 00:05:34,835
Something terrible must
have happened here.

135
00:05:43,135 --> 00:05:45,386
Oh, no, no, no, no...

136
00:05:48,307 --> 00:05:49,975
Stevie, I was never here.

137
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:56,481
ROSS:
Dad?

138
00:05:56,523 --> 00:05:57,482
I'm here.

139
00:05:57,524 --> 00:05:58,483
Hey.

140
00:05:58,525 --> 00:05:59,567
Hi.

141
00:05:59,609 --> 00:06:01,402
God, it seems like
just yesterday

142
00:06:01,444 --> 00:06:03,989
you guys used to come out
to watch me work.

143
00:06:04,031 --> 00:06:06,574
Dad, we can't believe
you're selling the house.

144
00:06:06,616 --> 00:06:09,577
Well, it's time for a new family
to start their memories here.

145
00:06:09,619 --> 00:06:11,370
And hopefully,
their check will clear

146
00:06:11,412 --> 00:06:13,540
before they find the crack
in the foundation

147
00:06:13,581 --> 00:06:15,167
and the asbestos in the ceiling.

148
00:06:17,002 --> 00:06:18,712
Let's just grab
our stuff--

149
00:06:18,753 --> 00:06:20,339
get the hell
out of here.

150
00:06:20,379 --> 00:06:23,717
I'm sorry we can't store
your childhood things anymore.

151
00:06:23,758 --> 00:06:25,802
I can't wait to see
everything again.

152
00:06:25,844 --> 00:06:27,179
All the memories.

153
00:06:27,221 --> 00:06:29,014
I don't know what's
in the boxes down here

154
00:06:29,056 --> 00:06:31,350
but I do know there are six
or seven Easy Bake Ovens

155
00:06:31,390 --> 00:06:32,600
in the attic.

156
00:06:32,642 --> 00:06:35,478
I used to love
to play "restaurant."

157
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:38,773
Not as much as you loved
to play "uncooked batter eater."

158
00:06:38,815 --> 00:06:41,568
Hey, it is unreasonable
to expect a child

159
00:06:41,609 --> 00:06:44,445
to wait for a light bulb
to cook brownies.

160
00:06:46,281 --> 00:06:48,909
So, I think your boxes
are over here.

161
00:06:48,950 --> 00:06:50,285
Oh, great.

162
00:06:50,327 --> 00:06:54,455
Hey, Dad,
whose cigarettes are these?

163
00:06:55,790 --> 00:06:57,751
I don't know.

164
00:06:57,792 --> 00:07:01,420
They... they must
be your mother's.

165
00:07:01,462 --> 00:07:04,174
But please, please
don't ask her.

166
00:07:04,216 --> 00:07:06,134
I'll throw these away.

167
00:07:10,138 --> 00:07:11,514
Cool, Dad!

168
00:07:11,556 --> 00:07:13,183
My report cards!

169
00:07:13,225 --> 00:07:15,102
Hey, check
this out, Dad...

170
00:07:15,143 --> 00:07:17,896
Math, "A," Science, "A"

171
00:07:17,938 --> 00:07:19,898
History, "A," Gym...

172
00:07:25,862 --> 00:07:33,078
Ooh, my rock polisher!

173
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:34,662
Uh-oh.

174
00:07:34,704 --> 00:07:36,081
What?

175
00:07:36,123 --> 00:07:37,791
You know how the garage
floods every spring?

176
00:07:37,832 --> 00:07:41,128
How are you ever
going to sell this place?

177
00:07:41,169 --> 00:07:44,298
I think I accidentally
used Monica's boxes

178
00:07:44,339 --> 00:07:47,384
to keep the water away
from the Porsche.

179
00:07:49,803 --> 00:07:51,888
Oh, no, Dad.

180
00:07:51,930 --> 00:07:53,265
Dad...

181
00:07:53,307 --> 00:07:55,392
What the...?

182
00:07:55,434 --> 00:07:58,561
Oh... Everything's ruined.

183
00:07:58,603 --> 00:08:01,022
Dad, she's going
to be crushed.

184
00:08:02,441 --> 00:08:04,985
You don't secretly smoke,
do you?

185
00:08:06,236 --> 00:08:07,695
No.

186
00:08:07,737 --> 00:08:10,365
So it's just your mother then.

187
00:08:13,827 --> 00:08:15,203
Hi.

188
00:08:15,245 --> 00:08:18,165
This is Phoebe from
Empire Office Supplies.

189
00:08:18,206 --> 00:08:21,293
Can I speak to your
supply manager, please?

190
00:08:21,335 --> 00:08:23,586
Earl. Thanks.

191
00:08:24,671 --> 00:08:26,047
Hi, Earl.

192
00:08:26,089 --> 00:08:28,800
This is Phoebe from
Empire Office Supplies.

193
00:08:28,842 --> 00:08:31,428
I'd like to talk to you
about your toner needs.

194
00:08:31,470 --> 00:08:34,931
( speaking in monotone ):
I don't need any toner.

195
00:08:34,973 --> 00:08:37,058
I'm hearing what you're saying

196
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:39,811
but at our prices,
everyone needs toner.

197
00:08:39,853 --> 00:08:42,647
Not me.

198
00:08:42,689 --> 00:08:44,774
May I ask why?

199
00:08:44,816 --> 00:08:47,526
You want to know why...

200
00:08:47,568 --> 00:08:49,570
You want to know why?

201
00:08:49,612 --> 00:08:51,614
I surely do.

202
00:08:51,656 --> 00:08:53,408
Okay.

203
00:08:53,450 --> 00:08:55,952
I don't need any toner...

204
00:08:56,995 --> 00:08:59,039
because I'm going
to kill myself.

205
00:09:09,216 --> 00:09:11,301
Um...

206
00:09:11,343 --> 00:09:13,970
Is-is that because
you're out of toner?

207
00:09:18,183 --> 00:09:22,437
Okay, so... no toner today.

208
00:09:22,479 --> 00:09:23,563
Thanks, anyway. Bye-bye.

209
00:09:23,604 --> 00:09:25,606
No! No! Wait! Wait!

210
00:09:25,648 --> 00:09:27,317
I can't just let you
hang up.

211
00:09:27,359 --> 00:09:28,943
Just... please talk to me.

212
00:09:28,985 --> 00:09:31,946
Well...

213
00:09:31,988 --> 00:09:33,740
I only had one thing
to do today.

214
00:09:36,535 --> 00:09:38,495
Guess I could push it back.

215
00:09:38,537 --> 00:09:41,331
Yeah. Now...

216
00:09:41,373 --> 00:09:44,501
why do you want
to kill yourself?

217
00:09:44,543 --> 00:09:47,462
I've been working
for ten years now

218
00:09:47,504 --> 00:09:50,298
at this meaningless,
dead-end job

219
00:09:50,340 --> 00:09:53,343
and nobody here
even knows that I exist.

220
00:09:53,385 --> 00:09:55,387
Chandler?

221
00:10:00,683 --> 00:10:01,726
I'm-I'm sorry?

222
00:10:01,768 --> 00:10:02,727
No.

223
00:10:02,769 --> 00:10:05,688
Oh... look...

224
00:10:05,730 --> 00:10:08,567
I'm-I'm sure that people
know you exist.

225
00:10:08,608 --> 00:10:09,568
Oh, yeah?

226
00:10:09,608 --> 00:10:10,777
I work in a cubicle

227
00:10:10,818 --> 00:10:12,737
surrounded
by people.

228
00:10:12,779 --> 00:10:14,239
I've been
talking to you

229
00:10:14,281 --> 00:10:16,991
for five minutes now
about killing myself...

230
00:10:17,033 --> 00:10:19,035
and no one's even looked up
from their desk.

231
00:10:19,077 --> 00:10:20,454
Hang on.

232
00:10:20,495 --> 00:10:22,080
Hey, everybody, uh...

233
00:10:22,122 --> 00:10:24,124
I'm going to kill myself.

234
00:10:28,962 --> 00:10:31,590
I got nothing.

235
00:10:33,632 --> 00:10:35,594
Good chair.

236
00:10:35,635 --> 00:10:39,639
Now, if anybody asks,
your name is Rosita.

237
00:10:44,769 --> 00:10:46,354
RACHEL:
Joey, you're going
to like it.

238
00:10:46,396 --> 00:10:47,355
JOEY:
No, I won't.

239
00:10:47,397 --> 00:10:48,607
How do you
even know?

240
00:10:48,648 --> 00:10:50,066
I know what I like
and what I don't like

241
00:10:50,108 --> 00:10:51,234
and it's not
the same chair.

242
00:10:51,276 --> 00:10:53,069
Joey, you didn't
even sit in it.

243
00:10:53,111 --> 00:10:55,447
I'm telling you,
it's such a nice chair.

244
00:10:55,489 --> 00:10:57,240
I feel bad
sitting in it so soon.

245
00:11:03,704 --> 00:11:05,248
Poor thing.

246
00:11:05,290 --> 00:11:07,000
Cut down in
her prime.

247
00:11:08,585 --> 00:11:10,920
Joey, the new chair
will be here in an hour.

248
00:11:10,962 --> 00:11:14,132
Maybe we should actually move
Rosita out of here, you know?

249
00:11:14,174 --> 00:11:16,217
Start the healing process?

250
00:11:16,259 --> 00:11:17,844
Yeah, I guess
you're right.

251
00:11:39,740 --> 00:11:41,742
She's healed!

252
00:11:46,247 --> 00:11:47,624
That's weird.

253
00:11:47,666 --> 00:11:51,252
No, it's not weird,
it's a miracle!

254
00:11:51,294 --> 00:11:52,920
It's not a miracle,
Joey.

255
00:11:52,962 --> 00:11:55,298
I'm sure there's
some explanation.

256
00:11:55,340 --> 00:11:56,299
Oh, there is.

257
00:11:56,341 --> 00:11:57,800
If you want
something enough

258
00:11:57,842 --> 00:11:59,678
and your heart is pure,
wondrous things can happen!

259
00:12:02,055 --> 00:12:03,348
Well, she'll
understand, right?

260
00:12:03,390 --> 00:12:04,807
It's not like I
did it on purpose.

261
00:12:04,849 --> 00:12:07,060
Dad, that won't
matter to her.

262
00:12:07,101 --> 00:12:09,688
Look, all my stuff
is safe and dry

263
00:12:09,728 --> 00:12:13,400
and all her stuff is-is...
is growing new stuff.

264
00:12:14,442 --> 00:12:16,236
See, this is exactly
the kind of thing

265
00:12:16,277 --> 00:12:18,029
that makes her think
you guys love me

266
00:12:18,071 --> 00:12:19,322
more than you love her.

267
00:12:19,364 --> 00:12:21,449
Oh, my God! Does she
really think that?

268
00:12:21,491 --> 00:12:22,576
Well, can you blame her?

269
00:12:22,617 --> 00:12:24,244
Well, I don't know.

270
00:12:24,285 --> 00:12:27,163
I-I suppose we may
have favored you
unconsciously.

271
00:12:27,205 --> 00:12:28,456
You were a
medical marvel!

272
00:12:28,498 --> 00:12:29,583
The doctors said your
mother could never...

273
00:12:29,624 --> 00:12:31,167
Dad, Dad, I don't want
to hear about it.

274
00:12:31,209 --> 00:12:31,918
Really?

275
00:12:31,959 --> 00:12:34,379
Well, not right now.

276
00:12:34,421 --> 00:12:36,673
Okay, look-- Monica came here
for some memories

277
00:12:36,715 --> 00:12:38,799
and, damn it, we're
going to give her some!

278
00:12:38,841 --> 00:12:40,801
Okay, grab...

279
00:12:40,843 --> 00:12:42,803
Grab some empty
boxes, okay?

280
00:12:42,845 --> 00:12:44,264
We'll-we'll take
stuff from mine

281
00:12:44,305 --> 00:12:45,932
and whatever we
can pass off as hers

282
00:12:45,973 --> 00:12:47,308
we'll-we'll put
them in there.

283
00:12:47,350 --> 00:12:48,309
Great.

284
00:12:48,351 --> 00:12:49,310
Like, uh...

285
00:12:49,352 --> 00:12:50,520
you know, like this.

286
00:12:50,562 --> 00:12:51,979
This-- she could've
made this, right?

287
00:12:52,021 --> 00:12:53,147
Sure.

288
00:12:53,189 --> 00:12:54,815
And this, she-she
could've won this.

289
00:12:54,857 --> 00:12:55,983
This could've
been hers.

290
00:12:56,025 --> 00:12:57,444
Sure.

291
00:12:57,485 --> 00:12:59,237
You know, if you
want to tell the
medical marvel

292
00:12:59,279 --> 00:13:02,115
story while we work,
that'd be fine.

293
00:13:05,118 --> 00:13:09,330
Oh! I am so psyched
I kept this chair
for myself!

294
00:13:11,458 --> 00:13:13,167
Oh!

295
00:13:13,209 --> 00:13:15,128
Yeah, me, too.

296
00:13:17,464 --> 00:13:18,757
Wow!

297
00:13:18,797 --> 00:13:20,592
You know that this thing
has speakers

298
00:13:20,634 --> 00:13:22,093
in the headrest?

299
00:13:22,135 --> 00:13:23,177
No. Really?

300
00:13:23,219 --> 00:13:24,679
Yeah!

301
00:13:24,721 --> 00:13:27,014
You can hook it up to your TV,
and you get radio.

302
00:13:32,145 --> 00:13:35,231
My chair heals itself.

303
00:13:36,857 --> 00:13:38,485
All right, so, Earl

304
00:13:38,526 --> 00:13:40,737
let's just forget about
the people at the office, okay?

305
00:13:40,779 --> 00:13:42,821
There-there's got to be
someone else in your life

306
00:13:42,863 --> 00:13:44,282
that's worth
sticking around for.

307
00:13:44,324 --> 00:13:46,743
What about... what about
your family or friends

308
00:13:46,785 --> 00:13:48,202
or maybe a girlfriend?

309
00:13:48,244 --> 00:13:50,330
( laughing ):
Yeah. Right.

310
00:13:50,371 --> 00:13:52,707
Oh, sorry. Boyfriend.

311
00:13:52,749 --> 00:13:54,709
Okay, I should...

312
00:13:54,751 --> 00:13:57,504
I should probably
be getting back to my thing now.

313
00:13:57,545 --> 00:13:58,505
See you.

314
00:13:58,546 --> 00:13:59,506
No...

315
00:13:59,547 --> 00:14:00,757
I'm not finished yet!

316
00:14:00,799 --> 00:14:02,509
Don't you dare hang up on me!

317
00:14:02,550 --> 00:14:04,469
The new girl's good.

318
00:14:07,888 --> 00:14:10,183
Okay, so, which boxes
are mine?

319
00:14:10,224 --> 00:14:11,768
Oh, these are
yours right here.

320
00:14:11,810 --> 00:14:13,394
Okay.

321
00:14:13,436 --> 00:14:15,313
Oh! A coloring book!
Yeah, yeah.

322
00:14:15,355 --> 00:14:16,939
Oh, you loved
that thing.

323
00:14:16,981 --> 00:14:18,692
You always had it
with you.

324
00:14:18,733 --> 00:14:21,944
You never went anywhere
without that coloring book.

325
00:14:21,986 --> 00:14:23,780
Really? Wow.

326
00:14:23,822 --> 00:14:26,199
Looks like I had some trouble
staying inside the lines.

327
00:14:26,240 --> 00:14:27,408
Nuh-uh!

328
00:14:29,577 --> 00:14:31,329
Wow, look at this!

329
00:14:31,371 --> 00:14:33,707
I can't believe I even
fit into this shirt.

330
00:14:37,251 --> 00:14:38,837
Oh...

331
00:14:38,877 --> 00:14:40,839
This is yours.

332
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:42,382
I don't know
how that got in there.

333
00:14:42,423 --> 00:14:43,633
( nervous chuckle )

334
00:14:43,675 --> 00:14:46,135
Hey, this...
this isn't
my stuff.

335
00:14:46,177 --> 00:14:48,805
Ross, these are your boxes.

336
00:14:53,934 --> 00:14:56,479
Where are my boxes?

337
00:14:56,521 --> 00:14:58,105
Well, the garage flooded,
sweetie

338
00:14:58,147 --> 00:15:00,316
and it ruined everything
in your boxes.

339
00:15:00,358 --> 00:15:01,609
I'm sorry.

340
00:15:01,651 --> 00:15:03,110
Just mine?

341
00:15:03,152 --> 00:15:04,904
I'm afraid so.

342
00:15:04,945 --> 00:15:08,408
So why... why wasn't
Ross's stuff ruined?

343
00:15:08,449 --> 00:15:12,537
I used your boxes to divert
water away from the Porsche.

344
00:15:12,579 --> 00:15:14,955
So, wait-- Ross's stuff is fine,
but I have no memories

345
00:15:14,997 --> 00:15:16,583
because you wanted to keep

346
00:15:16,624 --> 00:15:19,126
the bottom two inches
of your car away from water?

347
00:15:19,168 --> 00:15:21,962
There was also leaves
and gook and stuff.

348
00:15:23,715 --> 00:15:26,259
I can't believe this!

349
00:15:33,307 --> 00:15:35,309
Screw it.
I'm having one.

350
00:15:39,606 --> 00:15:41,023
Hey, Chandler.

351
00:15:41,065 --> 00:15:42,024
Hey.

352
00:15:42,066 --> 00:15:43,943
How would you like to sit

353
00:15:43,984 --> 00:15:46,279
in a chair that fully reclines

354
00:15:46,320 --> 00:15:50,241
has a rolling massage,
and speakers in the headrest?

355
00:15:50,283 --> 00:15:51,534
Yeah, I'd love to

356
00:15:51,576 --> 00:15:53,828
but I've tried that
so many times

357
00:15:53,870 --> 00:15:56,997
they won't even let me
in the store anymore.

358
00:15:57,039 --> 00:15:58,374
Well, what if I told you

359
00:15:58,416 --> 00:16:00,418
that you could do it
in my apartment?

360
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:03,379
Are you telling me
that you bought the chair

361
00:16:03,421 --> 00:16:06,633
that is making all other
lounge systems obsolete

362
00:16:06,674 --> 00:16:10,470
the chair that Sit Magazine
called, "Chair of the Year"?

363
00:16:10,511 --> 00:16:11,805
Mm-hmm.

364
00:16:11,846 --> 00:16:17,727
I just purchased
the La-Z-Boy Ecliner 3000.

365
00:16:17,769 --> 00:16:19,145
But that's awesome.
That's great.

366
00:16:19,186 --> 00:16:20,688
What made you do it?

367
00:16:20,730 --> 00:16:23,399
Well, it's a long story,
but, um, I broke Joey's chair.

368
00:16:23,441 --> 00:16:24,609
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

369
00:16:24,651 --> 00:16:26,319
You broke Joey's chair?

370
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:27,654
Yeah.
I thought I broke
Joey's chair.

371
00:16:27,695 --> 00:16:29,906
That's why I replaced it
with mine.

372
00:16:29,948 --> 00:16:30,990
Oh...

373
00:16:31,031 --> 00:16:33,992
that's how it got fixed.

374
00:16:34,034 --> 00:16:35,453
Well, what did you think

375
00:16:35,495 --> 00:16:37,622
that-that elves came in
and fixed it?

376
00:16:37,664 --> 00:16:38,623
No...

377
00:16:38,665 --> 00:16:39,624
( laughs )

378
00:16:39,666 --> 00:16:41,626
angels.

379
00:16:41,668 --> 00:16:43,419
I'm getting my chair back.

380
00:16:43,461 --> 00:16:45,379
Wha... w... hey.

381
00:16:47,298 --> 00:16:50,092
Well, looks like
it wasn't healed after all.

382
00:16:50,134 --> 00:16:51,093
Yeah.

383
00:16:51,135 --> 00:16:52,094
So...

384
00:16:52,136 --> 00:16:54,597
I guess this chair's mine now.

385
00:16:54,639 --> 00:16:55,974
( clears throat )

386
00:16:56,015 --> 00:16:57,684
( sighs pleasurably )

387
00:16:57,725 --> 00:16:59,686
Joey, you broke my chair!

388
00:16:59,727 --> 00:17:01,020
Your chair?

389
00:17:01,061 --> 00:17:02,689
Yeah, he thought he broke
your chair

390
00:17:02,730 --> 00:17:04,064
so he switched the chairs.

391
00:17:04,106 --> 00:17:05,984
So, there was no miracle?

392
00:17:06,024 --> 00:17:07,986
No, Joe, no miracle.

393
00:17:08,027 --> 00:17:10,237
Oh, no, this is devastating.

394
00:17:11,906 --> 00:17:13,240
My faith is shaken.

395
00:17:13,282 --> 00:17:14,742
I'm so glad I have the new chair
to get me

396
00:17:14,784 --> 00:17:19,038
through this difficult time
in my life.

397
00:17:19,079 --> 00:17:22,124
Excuse me, could you tell me
where I could find Earl?

398
00:17:22,166 --> 00:17:23,793
He's the supply manager here.

399
00:17:23,835 --> 00:17:25,294
Sorry,
I don't know any Earl.

400
00:17:25,336 --> 00:17:27,964
I'm right here!

401
00:17:30,967 --> 00:17:34,261
Earl...

402
00:17:34,303 --> 00:17:35,221
I'm Phoebe.

403
00:17:35,262 --> 00:17:37,223
Phoebe?

404
00:17:37,264 --> 00:17:39,350
The lady who sells toner?

405
00:17:39,392 --> 00:17:42,228
Look, you can't kill yourself.

406
00:17:42,269 --> 00:17:45,648
Look, um... I really appreciate
you coming down...

407
00:17:45,690 --> 00:17:47,525
No-no, I can't...
I can't let you do it.

408
00:17:47,567 --> 00:17:49,443
Why?

409
00:17:49,485 --> 00:17:51,905
Because it was fate
that made me call you today.

410
00:17:51,946 --> 00:17:54,032
I thought it was toner.

411
00:17:54,072 --> 00:17:55,033
No.

412
00:17:55,073 --> 00:17:56,701
Think about it, okay?

413
00:17:56,743 --> 00:17:59,662
This isn't even
my regular job, okay?

414
00:17:59,704 --> 00:18:02,582
And my first day on the job,
you're my first call

415
00:18:02,623 --> 00:18:05,209
an-and somebody else
might've hung up on you

416
00:18:05,251 --> 00:18:08,629
but I wouldn't do that,
because I know about this stuff.

417
00:18:08,671 --> 00:18:10,006
My mom killed herself.

418
00:18:10,048 --> 00:18:11,007
Really?

419
00:18:11,049 --> 00:18:12,508
Yes.
How?

420
00:18:12,550 --> 00:18:14,301
I'm not going to give you tips.

421
00:18:16,136 --> 00:18:17,346
Look, don't you see?

422
00:18:17,388 --> 00:18:19,724
Th-This-This...
this all came together

423
00:18:19,766 --> 00:18:22,226
so that I could stop you
from doing this.

424
00:18:22,268 --> 00:18:24,854
Couldn't it just
be a coincidence?

425
00:18:24,896 --> 00:18:26,856
No, it's fate.

426
00:18:26,898 --> 00:18:30,818
That doesn't really seem
like enough to be fate.

427
00:18:30,860 --> 00:18:35,364
Oh, well... um, okay,
here's a weird thing--

428
00:18:35,406 --> 00:18:38,993
my mother was also
a supply manager.

429
00:18:39,035 --> 00:18:41,913
Well, I'm actually
the office manager.

430
00:18:41,955 --> 00:18:44,415
Oh, my God, so was she!

431
00:18:44,457 --> 00:18:46,584
And, get this, okay?

432
00:18:46,626 --> 00:18:49,879
Your-Your name is Earl, right?

433
00:18:49,921 --> 00:18:53,007
Her name was Pearl, Puh-earl.

434
00:18:53,049 --> 00:18:55,802
W-Was there anything else?

435
00:18:55,843 --> 00:18:57,804
Sure.

436
00:18:57,845 --> 00:18:59,513
Um...

437
00:18:59,555 --> 00:19:00,848
where you from?

438
00:19:00,890 --> 00:19:01,515
Philadelphia.

439
00:19:01,557 --> 00:19:02,600
Oh, my God!

440
00:19:02,642 --> 00:19:03,684
So was she!

441
00:19:03,726 --> 00:19:05,352
( laughing )

442
00:19:05,394 --> 00:19:07,396
So, I've got goose...
I've got goose bumps.

443
00:19:07,438 --> 00:19:08,856
( chuckling ):
Really?

444
00:19:08,898 --> 00:19:10,232
Well, you know,
I'm wearing layers

445
00:19:10,274 --> 00:19:11,609
and it's warm, so...
Yeah, yeah.

446
00:19:11,651 --> 00:19:12,986
But it... look...

447
00:19:13,027 --> 00:19:15,863
Okay, these jerks might not care
about you

448
00:19:15,905 --> 00:19:19,241
but the universe does
and that says a lot.

449
00:19:21,077 --> 00:19:22,620
Did you hear that?

450
00:19:22,662 --> 00:19:25,873
I don't need you guys
to care about me

451
00:19:25,915 --> 00:19:28,709
because the
universe cares!

452
00:19:28,751 --> 00:19:30,753
The whole universe!

453
00:19:30,795 --> 00:19:32,880
( laughing tauntingly )

454
00:19:35,133 --> 00:19:39,762
I really wish they'd care
just a little bit, though.

455
00:19:41,514 --> 00:19:43,432
Oh, this is terrible.

456
00:19:43,474 --> 00:19:45,768
Everything is destroyed.

457
00:19:45,810 --> 00:19:47,269
Look at this.

458
00:19:47,311 --> 00:19:49,522
It obviously meant enough
for me to save it

459
00:19:49,563 --> 00:19:52,191
and I don't even know
what it is.

460
00:19:52,232 --> 00:19:55,820
Oh... it's still soft.

461
00:19:57,571 --> 00:20:01,034
What do you think
this is?

462
00:20:01,075 --> 00:20:03,077
Mon, I-I think
it was a mouse.

463
00:20:03,119 --> 00:20:04,078
( screams )

464
00:20:04,120 --> 00:20:05,079
( thuds )

465
00:20:05,121 --> 00:20:06,455
Oh! Oh, my God!

466
00:20:11,418 --> 00:20:12,545
How are you, honey?

467
00:20:12,586 --> 00:20:14,338
MONICA:
How do you think
I am?

468
00:20:14,380 --> 00:20:17,175
You've wrecked
all my childhood memories...

469
00:20:17,216 --> 00:20:19,010
you love Ross
more than me...

470
00:20:19,052 --> 00:20:21,846
and I just rubbed a dead mouse
on my face.

471
00:20:24,057 --> 00:20:28,519
Sweetheart, we love you
just as much as Ross.

472
00:20:28,561 --> 00:20:31,230
Now, I'm sorry about
everything that happened

473
00:20:31,271 --> 00:20:34,316
and I'll probably
never be able to
make it up to you

474
00:20:34,358 --> 00:20:35,735
but... here's a start.

475
00:20:39,113 --> 00:20:40,573
What's this?

476
00:20:40,614 --> 00:20:42,449
It's the key
to my Porsche.

477
00:20:42,491 --> 00:20:44,785
Well... the key
to your Porsche.

478
00:20:44,827 --> 00:20:47,038
What?

479
00:20:47,080 --> 00:20:49,582
What?!

480
00:20:49,623 --> 00:20:51,709
I've been thinking
of getting rid of it

481
00:20:51,751 --> 00:20:53,586
and I was driving
it the other day

482
00:20:53,627 --> 00:20:55,922
and I saw my reflection
in a store window.

483
00:20:55,963 --> 00:20:58,382
Your mother's right--
I do look like an ass.

484
00:20:58,424 --> 00:21:00,426
You're giving me
your Porsche?

485
00:21:00,467 --> 00:21:02,261
You're kidding me, right?

486
00:21:02,302 --> 00:21:05,431
W-W-Wai...
W-Wai-Wait a minute.

487
00:21:05,472 --> 00:21:07,808
I mean, a couple of
stupid boxes get wet

488
00:21:07,850 --> 00:21:09,435
and she gets a Porsche?!

489
00:21:09,476 --> 00:21:12,312
Why don't we take
it for a spin?

490
00:21:12,354 --> 00:21:13,564
All right!

491
00:21:13,606 --> 00:21:15,274
Wh-What about me?!

492
00:21:15,315 --> 00:21:17,443
I'm a medical marvel!

493
00:21:17,484 --> 00:21:21,488
[Captioned by
The Caption Center
WGBH Educational Foundation]

494
00:21:23,657 --> 00:21:25,118
Oh, yeah.

495
00:21:25,159 --> 00:21:30,581
( both sighing pleasurably )

496
00:21:30,623 --> 00:21:31,582
Hey, guys.
BOTH:
Hey.

497
00:21:31,624 --> 00:21:32,833
Do you know what happened

498
00:21:32,875 --> 00:21:35,211
to Chandler's Barcalounger?

499
00:21:35,253 --> 00:21:36,294
Oh, yeah, Joey broke it.

500
00:21:36,336 --> 00:21:37,379
He had to get rid of it.

501
00:21:37,421 --> 00:21:39,048
Are you kidding?

502
00:21:39,090 --> 00:21:42,802
I get a Porsche
and the Barcalounger's gone?

503
00:21:42,843 --> 00:21:45,096
This is the best day ever!

